==Swedish==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|sv|gmq-osw|dygn}}, {{m|gmq-osw|dyghn}}, {{m|gmq-osw|døgn}}, {{m|gmq-osw|døghn}}, from {{inh|sv|non|dœgn}}, from {{inh|sv|gem-pro|*dōgin-}}, alternative form of {{m|gem-pro|*dōg-}}. Cognate with {{cog|da|døgn}}, {{cog|nb|døgn}} and {{cog|nn|døgn}}. See also {{cog|non|dœgr}}, {{cog|nn|døger}} and {{cog|is|dægur}}.

===Noun===
{{sv-noun|n}}

# [[day]], [[nychthemeron]] (24 hours)
# [[day]] (specifically, the 24 hour period from midnight to the following midnight)

====Declension====
{{sv-infl-noun-n-zero}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|sv|dygna}}

====Usage notes====
* The difference between {{m|sv|dag}} and {{m-self|sv|dygn}}. Imagine it is around 3 o'clock on a Monday; if someone say:
** ''"Om 4 '''dagar''' är jag på stranden"'' ("In 4 days I am on the beach"), meaning that they will be on the beach on Friday.
** ''"Om 4 '''dygn''' är jag på stranden"'' ("In 4 days I am on the beach"), meaning that they will be on the beach around 3 o'clock on Friday.

